{
 "description": "Resuming a file from its last verified chunk (draft files_resume_draft1, docs/protocol.md 'Resuming files'). source: the file every case uses, byte i is (i * 31) mod 251, with its size and base64 SHA-256. prefixes: base64 SHA-256 of the first 'offset' bytes; a running hash continued with the rest gives the whole file's. resumePayload: a files.resume plaintext for the transfer 'transferId' of 'size' bytes; expect is the decoded offset and prefix or 'invalid' (the sender then answers offset 0, reason invalid); a valid offset is a positive multiple of 1,048,576 (a block) not above the size, or the size itself. ackPayload: a files.resume.ack plaintext answering a files.resume of 'proposed' bytes; expect is 'resume' (continue from proposed), 'restart' (from byte zero) or 'invalid' (cancel the file, discard the partial). 'plaintext' is serialised by each platform's own encoder and 'plaintextString' is used byte for byte; 'write': true means each platform's own encoder produces the same fields. receiverDecision: a kept partial's record (its on-disk bytes are the source's first 'partLength' bytes, with 'flipByte' inverted, absent when 'partExists' is false) and a resumable files.offer at 'nowMs'; with 'afterRestart' the record is reloaded and swept first. expect.resumeAt is the offset of the files.resume the receiver sends after re-reading and checking its partial, or null for files.accept (byte zero); dropsRecord, when present, says whether the record is gone after the decision. senderDecision: the sender's answer to a files.resume from its own file (the source with 'senderFlipByte' inverted, cut to 'senderLength'; the offer's size and SHA-256 are that file's): the files.resume.ack offset and reason, from the SHA-256 of the file's first 'offset' bytes (kept at every block while hashing it for the offer, or read again). retention: kept partials (label, peer, updatedMs, inUse, verified length when it matters) and a new one saved at new.updatedMs: the labels evicted, in order (expired first, then the oldest not in use over the limits: 4 per device, 16 and 20 GiB of verified bytes in all). folderAccept and entryOffer (designed, folders only, not built yet): the folders.accept 'partial' object against the manifest's entry sizes, and an entry's files.offer 'offset' against the receiver's partial.",
